  "Ranking Insights — Overall Keywords": "Page-1 visibility built steadily through H1, running from roughly 41.7K keywords in early January to a peak near 49K by mid-April, with Position 1 rankings climbing from ~6,150 to over 7,300 at the high point. Since late April the curve has cooled — Page 1 totals have pulled back to ~44K and Position 1 has given back some ground to ~7,100. This reads as a normal post-peak consolidation rather than a structural loss: Position 1 and Page 1 totals both remain well above the December baseline.",
  "Ranking Insights — Cyberglossary (Priority Keywords)": "The priority Cyberglossary set is the strongest performer in the account. Position 1 rankings have grown from 90 to 122 keywords, and Page-1 totals have held in the 410–490 range all year versus a baseline of 402 — meaning even the recent dip still sits above where the program started. This is a durable, compounding win for the glossary content strategy.",
  "Ranking Insights — Articles (Priority Keywords)": "Article-level priority keywords only began being tracked from mid-January, so trend reads are shorter, but the trajectory is positive: Page-1 totals have grown from 46 to 57–58 keywords and AI Overview presence has more than doubled, from 7 to 12–13 keywords. This is a smaller keyword set than Cyberglossary, but it's moving in the right direction on both classic rankings and AI visibility simultaneously.",
  "Ranking Insights — Cyberglossary (Overall Keywords)": "At the full Cyberglossary keyword level, Page-1 totals have grown from a 29.4K baseline to over 33K currently, after peaking near 34.5K in April. Position 1 rankings are up nearly 16% versus baseline (4,485 → 5,217). This confirms the priority-keyword gains are part of a broader lift across Cyberglossary, not an isolated pocket.",
  "Ranking Insights — Bing (Overall Keywords)": "Bing performance is the most volatile line in the report. Page-1 totals opened at 573 in early January, dropped into the 450–500 range through spring, and now sit at 472 — roughly flat-to-down versus baseline. Position 2-3 rankings have actually grown sharply (32 → 229), suggesting keywords are stacking just below the top of Page 1 rather than being lost outright. Worth monitoring, not escalating.",
  "Traffic Growth": "Organic traffic followed the same arc as rankings: a run-up from 495K to a February/March peak near 571K, followed by a pullback to 477K in the most recent week. The underlying content mix has shifted favorably — Cyberglossary organic traffic is up from 115K to 187K average and international-page traffic has held firm around 190–220K throughout, showing the content footprint is broader and healthier than the topline alone suggests.",
  "GSC Click Growth": "Search Console clicks are essentially flat year-to-date (1.30M baseline vs. 1.31M latest) but average position has improved meaningfully, from 9.1 down to 6.9 — a real ranking quality gain. Impressions ballooned to over 300M in March/April before normalizing back toward 165M. Rankings are getting stronger even where click volume hasn't yet caught up — common when position gains land just outside the highest-CTR band.",
  "Bing Traffic Growth": "Bing traffic and clicks have softened somewhat from baseline, but average position has improved sharply — from 9.1 down to 5.3 — the best position reading of the whole report. Position quality is outpacing the traffic figures right now, which is typically a leading indicator of traffic gains still to come.",
  "Featured Snippets": "Featured snippets are the one metric that has moved clearly backward: starting at 287, climbing to a high of 396 in March, then falling sharply through Q2 to settle in the 109–148 range. A snippet-recovery push — schema markup, answer-box formatting, definition-style content — should be a near-term priority alongside the ranking wins elsewhere.",
  "Website Performance Score": "Desktop performance has drifted down from a 68 baseline to the mid-50s most weeks (currently 59), while mobile has held roughly flat (48 → 33 most recently). Both scores bounce week to week, typical of PageSpeed measurements, but the desktop softening warrants a technical health check given its direct link to Core Web Vitals.",
  "Core Web Vitals": "Both desktop and mobile Core Web Vitals have logged a Fail status in every measured week since tracking began. This is the single most important technical item in this report: Core Web Vitals are a confirmed Google ranking factor, and a sustained Fail status is a ceiling on how far the ranking gains elsewhere can go. A CWV remediation sprint (LCP, INP, CLS) should be prioritized immediately.",
  "Search-Generated Engagement (AI Overviews)": "This is the standout growth story in the report. AI Overview keyword presence (SEMrush) has grown from 13,887 to 22,905 — up 65% versus baseline. Citation counts across every major AI assistant have grown in parallel: ChatGPT citations peaked near 13,000 in June (from 8,862), Gemini is up to 1,548 (from 1,237), and Claude citations have nearly doubled from 179 to 370 after peaking above 570. Fortinet's content is clearly earning a growing share of voice inside AI-generated answers.",
  "Bing AI Performance": "Bing's AI-surfaced citations have grown substantially since tracking began, from roughly 395K to 877K weekly citations, with cited pages growing from 6,004 to 7,041. Both metrics show the same volatility pattern — a strong spring build-up followed by a summer pullback — but both remain multiples above their starting point.",
  "AI Visibility %": "Fortinet's AI Visibility peaked at 66.2% in late March (up from a 56.1% Dec baseline) before a sharp May pullback to the high-40s, stabilizing near 46–47%. Palo Alto has partially recovered to 41.8% after a similar drop. Cato Networks and SentinelOne show steadily improving visibility (8.3% and 7.3% respectively), while Wiz.io collapsed to near zero from a 4.7% starting point.",
  "Share of Voice %": "Fortinet's Share of Voice peaked at 14.0% in late April before normalizing to 8–9% in recent weeks. Palo Alto holds a stable 7–8% share while Cisco sits near 5–6%. Cato Networks and SentinelOne have grown steadily from their March start to 1.5% and 1.4% respectively. Wiz.io Share of Voice dropped to 0% in mid-tracking and has remained there.",
  "Citation Rate %": "Fortinet's Citation Rate peaked at 12.9% in Q1 before dropping back to 7–8% in recent weeks. Palo Alto's citation rate has risen from 1.7% to 3.3%, partially closing the gap. Cisco's citation share has climbed from 1.1% to 1.6% as LLMs increasingly surface networking content. CrowdStrike shows a notable anomaly at Wk14 (4.0%) that reverted the following week.",
  "SASE": "Fortinet and Palo Alto trade the top SASE visibility position throughout the tracking window. Both brands sustained visibility above 90% through April, with Fortinet peaking near 97.7% and Palo Alto reaching 97.4%. Both experienced sharp drops in May — Fortinet to 69.9–75.6%, Palo Alto to 41.5–48.0% — as LLM training and ranking patterns shifted for this category.",
  "OT Security": "Fortinet dominates OT Security AI Visibility, holding above 86% through Wk20 and peaking at 95.5%. Palo Alto entered tracking at Wk10 and grew rapidly to 88.9% before the May reset. Both brands dropped significantly in late May — Fortinet to 41–54%, Palo Alto to 25–34% — reflecting a sector-wide recalibration in how AI assistants handle industrial security queries.",
  "ZTNA": "Palo Alto holds the top ZTNA visibility position for most of the tracking period, consistently above 90% through April. Fortinet climbed from 69.3% to a peak of 90.2% by Wk17 before dropping to 39–55% in the May reset. Palo Alto also declined sharply to 37–41%, though both brands show partial recovery in later weeks.",
